**SOLID.**
- **S - Single Responsibility:** a class has one reason to change. A `ReportService` that fetches data, formats it and emails it should be split.
- **O - Open/Closed:** extend behaviour without editing existing code, for example new discount types via a `DiscountStrategy` interface instead of an `if/else` chain.
- **L - Liskov Substitution:** subtypes must be usable wherever the base type is. A `Square` that breaks `Rectangle`'s setWidth/setHeight contract violates it.
- **I - Interface Segregation:** many small interfaces rather than one fat one (a `Printer` should not be forced to implement `fax()`).
- **D - Dependency Inversion:** depend on abstractions; inject them (`OrderService` takes a `PaymentGateway`, not `StripeClient`).

**Class relationships.**
- **Dependency:** uses temporarily (method parameter).
- **Association:** holds a reference (`Teacher` knows `Student`).
- **Aggregation:** whole-part, parts can exist independently (`Department` and `Teacher`).
- **Composition:** whole-part with owned lifetime (`House` and `Room`).
- **Inheritance/realisation:** is-a / implements.

**Patterns with use cases:** Strategy (payment or pricing rules), Factory (create objects by type), Observer (notifications), Singleton (use sparingly), Decorator (add features such as logging), Builder (many optional parameters).

**Builder (Java).**

```java
public final class User {
    private final String name, email; private final int age; private final String phone;
    private User(Builder b) { name = b.name; email = b.email; age = b.age; phone = b.phone; }
    public static Builder builder(String name, String email) { return new Builder(name, email); }

    public static final class Builder {
        private final String name, email;       // required
        private int age; private String phone;  // optional
        private Builder(String name, String email) { this.name = name; this.email = email; }
        public Builder age(int a) { this.age = a; return this; }
        public Builder phone(String p) { this.phone = p; return this; }
        public User build() {
            if (age < 0) throw new IllegalStateException("age");
            return new User(this);
        }
    }
}
// User u = User.builder("Asha", "a@x.com").age(22).build();
```

Why: avoids telescoping constructors, gives readable calls, validates in `build()`, and yields an **immutable** object.
